-- SUM-TYPE-FIELDS-SPEC §5.1: what a LATER CLAUSE of a multi-clause
-- `DECIDE`/`MEANS` group knows about its own columns.
--
-- A clause group is first-match-wins, so the body of clause 2 runs only for
-- values no earlier clause matched. That is a real narrowing, and it is the one
-- that makes the idiom below — write the exceptional constructors as their own
-- clauses, then read the field in the catch-all clause — legal without a
-- `CONSIDER`.
--
-- It is also the ONLY witness of that idiom in the tree. Nothing else in the
-- corpus combines a multi-clause group with a sum type carrying a
-- some-arms-only field, so if this file goes red nothing else will. The
-- refusals — the same shapes where the narrowing does NOT cover the read — are
-- `not-ok/tc/partial-projection-fallthrough.l4`.
--
-- Every case is ARMED with an `#EVAL`, because a narrowing that only satisfies
-- the checker is worth nothing: what these programs must be is SAFE.

DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    Landlord
    Tenant   HAS monthly_rent IS A NUMBER

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Owner
    Agent
    Renter   HAS rent IS A NUMBER

-- 1. THE CANONICAL IDIOM. Clause 1 consumes `Landlord`, the only other
--    constructor, so clause 2's binder can only be a `Tenant`. Note that `a` is
--    a FRESH name, which the desugarer compiles to a catch-all `WHEN a` inside
--    the synthesised fall-through — so the narrowing has to survive being
--    handed from the column to that binder.
GIVEN p IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE rent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE rent a        IS a's monthly_rent

#EVAL rent Landlord
#EVAL rent (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 2. THE SAME, WRITTEN WITH THE COLUMN'S OWN NAME. Reusing the GIVEN name is
--    the language's wildcard idiom: the desugarer emits no test and no
--    rebinding at all, so the read is on the column binder itself.
GIVEN p IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE rentAgain Landlord IS 0
DECIDE rentAgain p        IS p's monthly_rent

#EVAL rentAgain (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 3. NESTED FALL-THROUGHS. Three clauses means a fall-through inside a
--    fall-through; clause 3 must see BOTH earlier clauses' effect, not just the
--    nearest one.
GIVEN x IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE actorRent Owner IS 0
DECIDE actorRent Agent IS 1
DECIDE actorRent a     IS a's rent

#EVAL actorRent Owner
#EVAL actorRent Agent
#EVAL actorRent (Renter OF 900)

-- 4. THROUGH AN ALIAS. `b MEANS a` is resolved at the READ, so `b` reads at
--    whatever `a` is narrowed to here.
GIVEN p IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE viaAlias Landlord IS 0
DECIDE viaAlias a        IS b's monthly_rent
    WHERE
        b MEANS a

#EVAL viaAlias (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 5. TWO COLUMNS, and the earlier clause says nothing about the other one. Its
--    first column is the column-wildcard spelling, so it fires for every
--    `flag` — which is what makes its second column's `Landlord` genuinely
--    consumed. (Change that `flag` to `TRUE` and the group stops being safe:
--    that is case 6 of the not-ok file.)
GIVEN flag IS A BOOLEAN
      p    IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE dueFrom flag Landlord IS 0
DECIDE dueFrom flag a        IS a's monthly_rent

#EVAL dueFrom TRUE  Landlord
#EVAL dueFrom FALSE (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 6. A CLAUSE THAT BINDS THE PAYLOAD still consumes its constructor, as long as
--    it supplies exactly that constructor's arity of sub-patterns and every one
--    of them is certainly a variable binding. This is the shape you get the
--    moment the exceptional arm carries data, which on a sum type WITH FIELDS
--    is the normal case; without it the idiom would be refused for a fact that
--    is plainly true — `r` matches every `Tenant`, so only a `Landlord` reaches
--    clause 2.
DECLARE Side IS ONE OF
    Owner   HAS deposit      IS A NUMBER
    Renter  HAS monthly_rent IS A NUMBER

GIVEN sd IS A Side
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE held (Renter r) IS r
DECIDE held a          IS a's deposit

#EVAL held (Renter OF 1500)
#EVAL held (Owner OF 4000)

-- 7. A DON'T-CARE COLUMN THE CHECKER CANNOT ENUMERATE, spelled with a FRESH
--    binder. `m` is a name no constructor answers to, so the desugarer compiles
--    it to no test — clause 1 fires for every number paired with a `Landlord`,
--    exactly as it would with a `BOOLEAN` there. Whether the OTHER column's
--    type happens to be enumerable has no bearing on this one's meaning, and
--    for a while it decided the answer: this program was refused while the same
--    shape over a `BOOLEAN` (case 5) was accepted, and renaming `m` to `n` also
--    made it pass.
GIVEN n  IS A NUMBER
      p2 IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE owedOn m Landlord IS 0
DECIDE owedOn n a        IS a's monthly_rent

#EVAL owedOn 3 Landlord
#EVAL owedOn 3 (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 8. A DON'T-CARE COLUMN DESTRUCTURED AS A RECORD. `Wrap` is the only
--    constructor of its type, so `Wrap x` tests nothing: clause 1 fires for
--    every `Wrap` paired with a `Landlord`, and the second column's `Landlord`
--    is genuinely consumed. Naming the sole arm of a one-arm sum is the same
--    shape.
DECLARE Wrap HAS inner IS A NUMBER

GIVEN w IS A Wrap
      p3 IS A Party
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE fromWrap (Wrap x) Landlord IS 0
DECIDE fromWrap w        a        IS a's monthly_rent

#EVAL fromWrap (Wrap OF 1) Landlord
#EVAL fromWrap (Wrap OF 1) (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 9. THE COLUMN'S TYPE WRITTEN AS A SYNONYM. `Occupant` is `Party`, so the
--    constructors to be consumed are `Party`'s. The column universe is taken
--    with the checker's own `rigidHeadOf`, which expands synonyms (chains and
--    parameterised ones included) — matching the written type's head instead
--    put this column back under the blanket suppression, and the read died at
--    run time.
DECLARE Occupant IS Party

GIVEN o IS AN Occupant
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE synRent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE synRent o        IS o's monthly_rent

#EVAL synRent Landlord
#EVAL synRent (Tenant OF 1500)

-- 10. THE SUM TYPE DECLARED INSIDE A SECTION. A `§` heading gives every name
--     under it a second, section-qualified spelling that SHARES the original's
--     identity; the clause analysis must compare identities, not spellings, or
--     the flagship idiom is refused for a heading that changes nothing about
--     the program. (Kept last in the file because a heading is open-ended.)
§ `Occupancy`

DECLARE Lease IS ONE OF
    Vacant
    Occupied HAS rent_due IS A NUMBER

§ `Billing`

GIVEN l IS A Lease
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE billed Vacant IS 0
DECIDE billed x      IS x's rent_due

#EVAL billed Vacant
#EVAL billed (Occupied OF 250)
